s=read('cor-cm-linearization-error-bounds');s=deps(s,add=['def-cm-covariance-matrix-of-a-random-vector','lem-cm-linearized-propagation-of-covariance','lem-variance-and-covariance-identities-for-random-variables','thm-coordinatewise-measurability-into-r-n']);s=s.replace('Mean and mean-square bounds for the second-order linearization error','Mean, mean-square and covariance bounds for second-order linearization error');s=s.replace('dependency_level: 1','dependency_level: 2');insert=r'''
**Vector covariance error.** Let $f=(f_1,\ldots,f_d):U\to\mathbb R^d$, $d\ge1$, have $C^2$ components satisfying $\|H_{f_i}(x)\|\le B_i$ on $U$, $B_i\ge0$. Keep the same input hypotheses and assume $M_4:=E|X-\mu|^4<\infty$. Put $A=Df(\mu)$, $L=f(\mu)+A(X-\mu)$, $S=A\operatorname{Cov}(X)A^{\mathsf T}$ and $q_i=B_i^2 M_4/4$. Then $f(X)$ is square-integrable and for every $1\le i,j\le d$,
$$|\operatorname{Cov}(f(X))_{ij}-S_{ij}|\le\sqrt{S_{ii}q_j}+\sqrt{S_{jj}q_i}+\sqrt{q_iq_j}.$$
In particular, for the scalar function $h$ above, writing $s^2=\operatorname{Var}(h(\mu)+\nabla h(\mu)\cdot(X-\mu))$ and $q=B^2 M_4/4$ gives
$$|\operatorname{Var}(h(X))-s^2|\le2\sqrt{s^2q}+q=B\sqrt{s^2M_4}+\frac{B^2}{4}M_4.$$
No independence of input coordinates or remainders is assumed. These covariance bounds require the fourth moment; the second-moment mean bound alone does not control a covariance difference.

''';s=s.replace('## Facts & Assumptions',insert+'## Facts & Assumptions');s=s.replace('## Proof',r'''[L5] Square-integrable covariance is symmetric and bilinear, with $\operatorname{Var}(Z)=E[Z^2]-E[Z]^2\le E[Z^2]$. The affine linearization has covariance $A\operatorname{Cov}(X)A^{\mathsf T}$. ([[lem-variance-and-covariance-identities-for-random-variables]], [[lem-cm-linearized-propagation-of-covariance]], [[def-cm-covariance-matrix-of-a-random-vector]])

[L6] Coordinatewise measurability is equivalent to measurability into $\mathbb R^d$. ([[thm-coordinatewise-measurability-into-r-n]])

## Proof''');start=s.index('4.1 Steps');s=s[:start]+r'''4.1 In the vector case apply steps 1.1–3.2 componentwise to $f_i$ and put $R_i=f_i(X)-L_i$. Each $R_i$ is measurable and $E[R_i^2]\le q_i$. Each $L_i$ is square-integrable as a finite linear combination of the square-integrable coordinates of $X$: $(\sum_{k=1}^m a_kv_k)^2\le(\sum_{k=1}^m a_k^2)|v|^2$. Thus $f_i(X)=L_i+R_i$ is square-integrable by $(u+v)^2\le2u^2+2v^2$. Coordinatewise measurability gives a random vector $f(X)$, and [L5] gives $\operatorname{Cov}(L)=S$ and $\operatorname{Var}(R_i)\le q_i$. [given, step 1.1, step 3.2, L5, L6, algebra]

4.2 For any square-integrable real variables $V,W$, center them as $V_0=V-E[V]$, $W_0=W-E[W]$, and write $a=E[V_0^2]$, $b=E[W_0^2]$, $c=E[V_0W_0]$. Products are integrable by $2|V_0W_0|\le V_0^2+W_0^2$. Monotonicity and linearity give $0\le E[(V_0+tW_0)^2]=a+2tc+t^2b$ for every real $t$. If $b>0$, take $t=-c/b$ to obtain $c^2\le ab$; if $b=0$, the affine expression $a+2tc$ is nonnegative for all $t$, forcing $c=0$. Consequently $|\operatorname{Cov}(V,W)|\le\sqrt{\operatorname{Var}(V)\operatorname{Var}(W)}$, including zero variances. [L4, L5, algebra]

4.3 By covariance bilinearity, $\operatorname{Cov}(f_i(X),f_j(X))-S_{ij}=\operatorname{Cov}(L_i,R_j)+\operatorname{Cov}(R_i,L_j)+\operatorname{Cov}(R_i,R_j)$. Apply step 4.2 to these three terms and use $\operatorname{Var}(L_i)=S_{ii}$ and $\operatorname{Var}(R_i)\le q_i$ from step 4.1. The triangle inequality yields $|\operatorname{Cov}(f(X))_{ij}-S_{ij}|\le\sqrt{S_{ii}q_j}+\sqrt{S_{jj}q_i}+\sqrt{q_iq_j}$. [step 4.1, step 4.2, L5, algebra]

5.1 Steps 3.1 and 3.2 establish the scalar mean and mean-square conclusions; step 4.3 proves the full vector covariance conclusion, and setting $d=1$ with $B_1=B$ gives $2\sqrt{s^2q}+q=B\sqrt{s^2M_4}+B^2M_4/4$. [step 3.1, step 3.2, step 4.3, algebra] ∎
''';save('cor-cm-linearization-error-bounds',s)
