header={'id':ID,'kind':'lemma','title':'Finite IID sample-mean variance and Chebyshev bound','status':'draft','origin':'pipeline','domain':'mathematics','deps':sp,'dependency_roles':{i:'mathematical-premise' for i in sp},'dependency_level':1,'proof_strategy':'direct','provenance':{'statement':'ai-altered','proof':'ai-generated'},'sources':{'references':[{'title':'Rick Durrett, Probability: Theory and Examples, 5th ed., Section 2.2.1: finite variance sum and Chebyshev argument','url':'https://sites.math.duke.edu/~rtd/PTE/PTE5_011119.pdf'}]},'verification':{'precheck':'pass'}}
body=r'''
## Statement

For a finite repeated-observation model of [[def-cm-iid-sampling-model]] with $n\ge1$ IID square-integrable real random variables $Y_1,\ldots,Y_n$ on one probability space, common mean $\mu$ and variance $\sigma^2$, put $\overline Y=n^{-1}\sum_{k=1}^nY_k$. Then
$$E[\overline Y]=\mu,\qquad E|\overline Y-\mu|^2=\frac{\sigma^2}{n},\qquad \mathbb P(|\overline Y-\mu|\ge a)\le\frac{\sigma^2}{na^2}\quad(a>0).$$
No extension to an infinite IID sequence is required; no convergence assertion is made for a single finite family.

## Facts & Assumptions

**Given:** The finite IID family and hypotheses in the Statement.

[L1] The finite sampling model adopts IID and finite second moment, and its variables have common mean and variance. ([[def-cm-iid-sampling-model]])

[L2] For independent integrable variables, expectations of products factor; for the identity functions, $E[Y_iY_j]=E[Y_i]E[Y_j]$ at distinct indices. ([[thm-factorization-of-expectations-for-independent-variables]])

[L3] Covariance is bilinear on finite square-integrable combinations and $\operatorname{Cov}(V,W)=E[VW]-E[V]E[W]$. ([[lem-variance-and-covariance-identities-for-random-variables]])

[L4] Expectations of finite integrable linear combinations are linear. ([[cor-expectation-linearity-monotonicity-and-modulus-bound]])

[L5] If $Z$ is square-integrable and $a>0$, then $\mathbb P(|Z-E[Z]|\ge a)\le\operatorname{Var}(Z)/a^2$. ([[cor-chebyshev-inequality-for-random-variables]])

## Proof

1.1 Square-integrability makes each $Y_k$ integrable by $2|Y_k|\le1+Y_k^2$, and the finite mean is square-integrable by $(\sum_k y_k)^2\le n\sum_k y_k^2$. Therefore [L4] gives $E[\overline Y]=n^{-1}\sum_k\mu=\mu$. [given, L1, L4, algebra]

2.1 For $i\ne j$, mutual independence and [L2] give $E[Y_iY_j]=\mu^2$, hence $\operatorname{Cov}(Y_i,Y_j)=0$ by [L3]. Bilinearity gives $\operatorname{Var}(\overline Y)=n^{-2}\sum_{i,j}\operatorname{Cov}(Y_i,Y_j)=n^{-2}\sum_{i=1}^n\sigma^2=\sigma^2/n$. Together with step 1.1 this is $E|\overline Y-\mu|^2=\sigma^2/n$. [given, step 1.1, L2, L3, algebra]

3.1 Apply [L5] to $\overline Y$ using steps 1.1 and 2.1 to obtain the stated probability bound for every $a>0$. The finite calculation includes $n=1$ and $\sigma^2=0$ without dividing by a variance. [step 1.1, step 2.1, L5, algebra] ∎
''';save(ID,'---\n'+yaml.safe_dump(header,sort_keys=False,allow_unicode=True)+'---\n'+body)
s=read('ex-cm-math-evidence-pendulum-propagation');s=deps(s,add=['cor-cm-linearization-error-bounds','lem-cm-uniform-second-order-remainder-bound','def-hessian-and-euclidean-critical-point','thm-cauchy-schwarz-in-an-inner-product-space']);s=s.replace('dependency_level: 2','dependency_level: 3');s=s.replace('## Facts & Assumptions',r'''**Explicit bounded nonlinearity correction.** In addition suppose $X=(T,l)$ takes values in the open convex box $U_B=(T_0-\rho_T,T_0+\rho_T)\times(l_0-\rho_l,l_0+\rho_l)$, where $0<\rho_T<T_0$ and $\rho_l>0$. The coordinates are fixed numerical coordinates (no addition of physical durations and lengths is intended). Write $t_-=T_0-\rho_T$, $l_+=|l_0|+\rho_l$, and
$$H_h(T,l)=\begin{pmatrix}24\pi^2l/T^4&-8\pi^2/T^3\\-8\pi^2/T^3&0\end{pmatrix},\qquad B=\sqrt{(24\pi^2l_+/t_-^4)^2+2(8\pi^2/t_-^3)^2}.$$
Then $\|H_h\|\le B$ on $U_B$. For $R=h(X)-h(\mu)-Dh(\mu)(X-\mu)$, $M_k=E|X-\mu|^k$ and the propagated variance $s^2=Dh(\mu)\operatorname{Cov}(X)Dh(\mu)^{\mathsf T}$ computed above,
$$|R|\le\frac B2|X-\mu|^2,\qquad |E[R]|\le\frac B2M_2,\qquad E[R^2]\le\frac{B^2}{4}M_4,\qquad |\operatorname{Var}(h(X))-s^2|\le B\sqrt{s^2M_4}+\frac{B^2}{4}M_4.$$\nThe box makes $M_4\le(\rho_T^2+\rho_l^2)^2<\infty$. This correction is mathematical; physical small-angle and apparatus discrepancies are additional inputs of a physical consumer.

## Facts & Assumptions''');s=s.replace('## Verification',r'''[L5] On an open convex domain with $\|H_h\|\le B$, the Taylor remainder has $|R|\le B|X-\mu|^2/2$; its mean and mean-square obey the stated bounds, and its variance differs from the linearized variance by at most $B\sqrt{s^2M_4}+B^2M_4/4$ when $M_4<\infty$. ([[lem-cm-uniform-second-order-remainder-bound]], [[cor-cm-linearization-error-bounds]])

[L6] The Hessian is the matrix of second partial derivatives. For any matrix $H$ and vector $v$, applying Euclidean Cauchy–Schwarz to each row gives $|Hv|^2\le(\sum_{i,j}H_{ij}^2)|v|^2$, so its operator norm is at most the square root of the sum of squared entries. ([[def-hessian-and-euclidean-critical-point]], [[thm-cauchy-schwarz-in-an-inner-product-space]])

## Verification''');s=s.replace('[step 2.1, algebra] ∎','[step 2.1, algebra]');s+=r'''
4.1 Differentiating [L3] once more gives $h_{TT}=24\pi^2l/T^4$, $h_{Tl}=h_{lT}=-8\pi^2/T^3$ and $h_{ll}=0$, hence the displayed Hessian by [L6]. On $U_B$, $T>t_->0$ and $|l|<l_+$; the sum of squared entries is at most $(24\pi^2l_+/t_-^4)^2+2(8\pi^2/t_-^3)^2$, so [L6] gives $\|H_h\|\le B$. [given, L3, L6, algebra]

4.2 Under the additional box hypothesis, $|X-\mu|^2<\rho_T^2+\rho_l^2$ pointwise, so $M_4\le(\rho_T^2+\rho_l^2)^2<\infty$. The box is open and convex and contains $\mu$ and all values of $X$, so [L5] with step 4.1 gives the pointwise, mean, mean-square and scalar covariance-error bounds displayed in the Example. [given, step 4.1, L5, algebra]

5.1 Steps 2.1 and 3.1 establish the exact propagated covariance of the linearization for the initial square-integrable model; steps 4.1 and 4.2 establish its explicit Hessian correction under the additional box hypothesis. [step 2.1, step 3.1, step 4.1, step 4.2] ∎
''';save('ex-cm-math-evidence-pendulum-propagation',s)
